# Normalise line endings so a Windows checkout cannot commit a CRLF shell
# script or a CRLF version string.
#
# This is not cosmetic here. Two CI jobs parse these files on Linux:
#   - ci.yml -> release-scripts runs `bash -n scripts/release/*.sh`. A single
#     CR turns a valid script into a syntax error, or worse, silently changes
#     a here-doc terminator.
#   - release.yml -> verify compares the version in Cargo.toml against the
#     `v*` tag. A trailing CR makes "0.1.0\r" != "0.1.0" and the release
#     fails closed for no real reason.
#
# Until this file existed the repo happened to be clean only because every
# contributor had `core.autocrlf=input` (or `true`) configured locally. That
# is not a property of the repository, it is a property of the machines.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries — never line-ending translated.
*.png   binary
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.exe   binary
*.msi   binary
*.dmg   binary
*.AppImage binary
*.sig   binary

# Signature files are opaque bytes, not text.
*.sig binary

# Test fixtures for the mock R2 bucket are tarballs.
*.tar.gz binary

# Lockfiles: never normalize. A rewritten lockfile produces a diff that
# defeats `bun install --frozen-lockfile` and makes Cargo re-resolve.
bun.lock     -text
Cargo.lock   -text

# Windows-only scripts, which genuinely need CRLF to execute.
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf